    targetMuscles: "Transversus abdominis and pelvic floor\nObliques, erector spinae, glutes and hamstrings isometrically to stabilize the torso/pelvis during the hinge\nRectus abdominis/obliques concentrically to flex, eccentrically as the spine extends\nErector spinae concentrically to extend\nObliques to prevent lumbar overextension\nLatissimus dorsi to maintain arm pressure against the bar\nGlutes/hamstrings concentrically to draw the pelvis under\nScapular stabilizers",
    focus: "Stabilize the pelvis on the femurs during the initial hinge so the movement comes from the knee joints\nTry to maximize spinal flexion and minimize hip flexion at the start of the roll forward\nKeep the shoulders above the level of the bar as the spine extends so scapular stabilization can be maintained\nKeep the hamstrings active for balance but allow them to lengthen as the spine extends so the pelvis can anteriorly tilt slightly and the lumbar can extend\nWhen returning from extension and initiating flexion from the tail, allow the upper body to respond naturally rather than holding the head still (which causes excessive cervical extension)",
