    targetMuscles: "Transversus abdominis and pelvic floor\nScapular stabilizers throughout\nSpecific prime movers vary by position — see positions below.",
    focus: "Maintain scapular stabilization with each new arm or torso movement\nInitiate by rolling the ASIS away from the front of the femurs, increasing lumbar flexion with abdominals rather than leaning back\nAvoid overly flexing the cervical spine and jamming the chin into the chest\nKeep the wrists as long as possible without 'breaking'\nFor straight back, stabilize the spinal position as the pelvis hinges back — avoid flexing or extending the spine — and stabilize the scapulae especially as the hands reach up overhead",
    positions: [
      { label: "Round Back", detail: "Arms held out in front just below shoulder level, gently rounded, holding handles in fists, palms facing the torso. Pelvis rolls away from the front of the femurs into a C-curve; spine rolls into a C-curve (lumbar flexes as low as the abdominals stay flat, upper spine responding) as the elbows flex to draw the hands to the sternum; stays flexed as the elbows extend, palms turning to face back, hands reaching to the sides; then the torso flexes further forward over the legs as the arms press back behind the shoulders; torso stays flexed forward as the arms circle up and out to the sides, then reach forward over the feet; sequentially lengthens tail to head back to neutral as the arms return. Breathing: exhale, initiating by rolling the ASIS away from the femurs, to flex the lumbar as low as the abdominals stay flat, simultaneously flexing the elbows to draw the hands to the sternum; inhale, spine staying flexed, humeri still, to extend the elbows and turn the palms to face back, reaching hands to the sides; exhale, initiating with the abdominals, to flex the torso forward over the legs (weight on top of sit-bones) while pressing the arms back behind the shoulders, palms facing each other; inhale, torso staying flexed forward, to circle the arms up and out to the sides as high as scapular stabilization allows; exhale, maintaining transversus engagement, to reach the arms all the way forward over the feet, palms facing out; inhale to sequentially lengthen the spine tail to head back to neutral while the arms return to start. 5 reps. Targets rectus abdominis/obliques concentrically and isometrically to create/maintain flexion, glutes/hamstrings concentrically to bring the pelvis under, hip flexors eccentrically on roll down/concentrically on flexion forward, posterior deltoids concentrically to press the arms back, latissimus dorsi/teres major eccentrically as the arms circle forward." },
      { label: "Straight Back", detail: "Arms long, held out in front level with the shoulders, hands in straps, palms facing up, wrists/fingers long. Pelvis hinges away from and toward the femurs while the spine stays neutral, then flexes forward over the legs; spine stays neutral through the hinge back and forward, then flexes forward over the legs as the hands press down/back, stays flexed as arms circle up/out then reach forward, sequentially lengthens back to neutral as arms return. Breathing: inhale to hinge the pelvis away from the femurs as far as possible with neutral spine, simultaneously flexing the elbows to draw hands toward the ears; exhale, spine stable, to hinge the pelvis toward the femurs (weight on top of sit-bones) while extending the arms up and forward on a diagonal, palms facing each other, controlling the springs; inhale to continue hinging toward the femurs as far as possible, spine/arms staying long; exhale to flex the spine forward over the legs (transversus engaged) while pressing the hands down and back behind the shoulders; inhale, torso flexed over the legs, to circle the arms up/out to the sides then reach forward over the feet, palms facing out; exhale to sequentially lengthen the spine tail to head, returning to neutral as the arms return to start. 5 reps. Targets rectus abdominis/obliques/erector spinae to stabilize the spine during the hinge, biceps concentrically to flex the elbows then eccentrically as arms reach overhead, rectus abdominis/obliques concentrically to flex the spine, latissimus dorsi/teres major/posterior deltoid concentrically to extend the shoulders eccentrically as arms circle forward. Modification — Spinal extension: hinge the pelvis away from the femurs while extending the thoracic spine as the elbows flex to draw the hands to the ears, then return spine to neutral as the pelvis hinges toward the femurs and arms extend up/forward." },
    ],
    cues: ["Roll into a deep C-curve, head slightly higher than the pelvis", "Half-circle the arms out to the sides, reaching forward"],
    faults: ["Losing independent control of each arm — with independent handles, one side can dominate; work to make both arms move equally and at the same speed"],
    modifications: [
      "Alternate sitting positions — flex the knees slightly or sit on a foam cushion/platform extender if neutral pelvis cannot be achieved with legs straight",
    ],
    notes: "Progression from Essential Back Rowing prep (pg 76).\nBecause each arm spring is independent, the exercise requires more control from the stabilizing muscles than a single connected resistance would, and challenges both arms to work equally and at the same speed.\nOn the Cadillac, the height of the sliding bar can be adjusted to change spring resistance and target different muscles depending on where it is set (a higher sliding bar increases the shoulder-adductor challenge)\nThis is busier than the Reformer version since there is no carriage sliding through the range to help.",
  
    script: { lines: ["Let's move into Back Rowing. Come to seated, facing the vertically sliding bar, legs straight and together.", "We'll work through Round Back, then Straight Back, one full arm and torso sequence per position, five reps each.", "Roll into a deep C-curve with your head slightly higher than your pelvis, and keep both arms moving with equal weight and speed — the independent springs make it easy for one side to dominate. Feel your posterior deltoids and latissimus dorsi driving the arm work and your abdominals supporting the spine."] },
